============================================================
README file for $(corelib)/progs/students/xinwei

Core Library (since version 2.1, March 2012)
============================================================

Description:
	This directory has various subdirectoris containing algorithms
	for robotics, especially motion planning.

	Subdirectory 3d-sphere:
	This is a 3d adapation of $(corelib)/progs/robotics/2d-disk.

Author:
	Xinwei Lin (2013).

$Id: README $
============================================================
Files:
	README		-- this file

Directories:
	K_3/cmpr1, K_3/cmpr2, ..., K_3/cmpr5	-- This directory
				has various environments for motion planning
	K-3		-- motion planning for a disc in the plane

============================================================
COMMENTS ON CODE:
	The main program is Draw.cpp.
	
	COMMAND Line argument is:
		./Draw ${filename} ${runtype} \
		    ${spaceradius} ${epsilon} ${sphereradius} \
		    ${xStart} ${yStart} ${zStart} \
		    ${xEnd} ${yEnd} ${zEnd} \
		    &
	where	
		filename = directory name (e.g., cmpr or cmpr5) with 3 files called
				obstacles.txt, spacecfg.txt, sphereloc.txt
			describing the obstacles, start and goal config,
			radius of sphere, epsilon, etc.
		runtype = 0, 1 or 2
			These corresponds to BFS, random, Dijkstra strategies
			(see "runCommandCore" routine in Draw.cpp)
		spaceradius = dimension of the ROI
		epsilon = eps parameter
		sphereradius = robot radius
		
	E.g., in cmpr,
		obstacles.txt:
			description of the obstacles given by a list of triangles
			in this case, a tetrahedron
		sphereloc.txt has
			3		(robot radius)
			-20 -20 -20	(start cfg)
			20 20 20	(goal cfg)
		spacecfg.txt has
			epsilon=2	(epsilon parameter)
			maxRadius=50	(space radius, i.e., ROI radius)
	


			
